    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into a learned but declining branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, Murasaki learned classical Chinese by listening to her brother's lessons, though Heian women were traditionally excluded from it.</li><li>She began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a> while married or soon after her husband's death in 1001, and added scenes from court life after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-michinaga">Fujiwara no Michinaga</a> brought her to serve Empress Shōshi in about 1005.</li><li>Her diary, her poems and Genji are the main record of her life, and the dates of her death, c. 1014 or as late as 1025, remain disputed among scholars.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Murasaki Shikibu is a descriptive name, not a personal one: &quot;Shikibu&quot; refers to the Ministry of Ceremonials where her father worked, and &quot;Murasaki&quot; was most probably given to her at court after the character of that name in Genji.</p></aside>
    </section>

